(Bug #83: `not` was only a primary operator, so `If not heat is limit then,`
 compiled as `(not heat) is limit` - `0 is 6` - and the guard was false
 whatever the operands. LANGUAGE.md writes the operator as
 `not <condition>`, so `not` takes scope over the whole comparison after
 it, exactly as English does: "if it is not the case that heat is limit".)

a number called heat is 3.
a number called limit is 6.
a list called readings is [].

If not heat is limit then,
    Print "equality: fires".
Otherwise,
    Print "equality: silent".

If not heat is greater than 5 then,
    Print "ordering: fires".
Otherwise,
    Print "ordering: silent".

If not readings is empty then,
    Print "emptiness: fires".
Otherwise,
    Print "emptiness: silent".

(A property reads the same under either binding, so it is pinned here to
 keep it that way.)
If not heat is even then,
    Print "property: fires".
Otherwise,
    Print "property: silent".

(The negation is of the comparison, so an equal pair is silent and an
 unequal pair fires - the operands decide the answer, which was the whole
 complaint.)
a number called depth is 3.
If not heat is depth then,
    Print "equal pair: fires".
Otherwise,
    Print "equal pair: silent".
